Q: PowerShell: Possible to Determine a File&#39;s MIME Type? A: <p>windows CAN guess the mimetype and file ext by file data in powershell ...
This is not exactly what was requested, but I got this ready and in case anyone needs it, feel free to strip anything uneeded.</p>
<ol>
<li>it oeads the urlmon.dll (yes its old and outdated on magic bytes)</li>
<li>it tries to find the magic bytes with FindMimeFromData and gets the mimetype</li>
<li>it checks the registry for the extension (using the found mimetype)</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>Function script:Invoke-MimeSnifferLoaded {
    # Check if the Type exists in any currently loaded assembly
    $isLoaded = [AppDomain]::CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies() | 
                ForEach-Object { $_.GetType(&quot;Win32.MimeSniffer&quot;) } | 
                Where-Object { $null -ne $_ }

    if (-not $isLoaded) {
        Write-Verbose &quot;Loading MimeSniffer into memory...&quot;
        Add-Type -MemberDefinition @&quot;
            [DllImport(&quot;urlmon.dll&quot;, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode, ExactSpelling = true, SetLastError = false)]
            public static extern int FindMimeFromData(
                IntPtr pBC,
                [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPWStr)] string pwzUrl,
                [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPArray, ArraySubType=UnmanagedType.I1, SizeParamIndex=3)] byte[] pBuffer,
                int cbSize,
                [MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPWStr)] string pwzMimeProposed,
                int dwMimeFlags,
                out IntPtr ppwzMimeOut,
                int dwReserved);
&quot;@ -Name &quot;MimeSniffer&quot; -Namespace &quot;Win32&quot;
    }
}
function Get-FileTypeFromBytesWindows {
    param (
        [Parameter(Mandatory=$true)]
        [byte[]]$Content
    )

    if ($Content.Length -eq 0) { return $null }

    # Quick Magic Bytes check, also fixes JPEG + EXIF not being detected by windows
    switch -Regex ($null) {
        # JPEG: FF D8 FF
        { $Content[0] -eq 0xFF -and $Content[1] -eq 0xD8 -and $Content[2] -eq 0xFF } { return &quot;jpg&quot; }

        # PNG: 89 50 4E 47
        { $Content[0] -eq 0x89 -and $Content[1] -eq 0x50 -and $Content[2] -eq 0x4E -and $Content[3] -eq 0x47 } { return &quot;png&quot; }
    }

    script:Invoke-MimeSnifferLoaded

    # 1. Use the Windows API to detect the MIME type from bytes
    $bufferSize = [Math]::Min($Content.Length, 256)
    $ptr = [IntPtr]::Zero

    # Extract ONLY the first 256 bytes for the API
    [byte[]]$buffer = New-Object byte[] $bufferSize
    [Array]::Copy($Content, $buffer, $bufferSize)

    $result = [Win32.MimeSniffer]::FindMimeFromData([IntPtr]::Zero, $null, $buffer, $bufferSize, $null, 0, [ref]$ptr, 0)

    $mimeType = $null
    if ($result -eq 0 -and $ptr -ne [IntPtr]::Zero) {
        $mimeType = [System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::PtrToStringUni($ptr)
        [System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal]::FreeCoTaskMem($ptr)
    }

    # If the Windows API fails (e.g. EXIF JPEGs), return null. Magic Bytes fallback was done before anyways
    if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($mimeType) -or $mimeType -eq &quot;application/octet-stream&quot;) {
        return $null
    }

    # 2. Look up the extension associated with this MIME type in the Windows Registry
    $regPath = &quot;Registry::HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\MIME\Database\Content Type\$mimeType&quot;
    if (Test-Path $regPath) {
        $extension = (Get-ItemProperty -Path $regPath -Name &quot;Extension&quot; -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).Extension
        if ($extension) {
            return $extension.TrimStart('.')
        }
    }

    return $null
}
</code></pre>
<p>Checking if the mimetype exists yourself:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>ls &quot;Registry::HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\MIME\Database\Content Type\&quot; |? Name -like &quot;*jpeg*&quot;
</code></pre>

